That's a problem: lock released before commit. Thread C (new lock object) reads DB → row not yet committed → not visible → C inserts placeholder → blocks on H2 row lock until A commits → DuplicateKeyException → read → replay. OK, the DB backstop handles it. But with H2, will the insert block or fail immediately? If it fails immediately with DuplicateKeyException while A uncommitted, then findById returns empty → orElseThrow conflict 409 — wrong-ish but it's an edge case (same requestId concurrent). Hmm, "唯一键竞争给业务结果" requires the race loser to get the business result. If H2 throws immediately and row not visible, we can't get the result. Add bounded retry: on duplicate, poll findById up to ~3s (sleep 25ms) until row with response_body appears → replay; if identity mismatch → 409 immediately.

Actually wait — does the in-JVM striped lock suffice for tests? All test concurrency goes through the same JVM/application context, so the striped lock serializes same-requestId requests fully... except the lock is removed in finally before commit. Fix: don't remove the lock eagerly — or better, keep removal but make it safe: remove only when no one waiting? Can't know. Alternative: never remove (unbounded growth — requestIds are unbounded → memory leak). Use Guava-style striped locks: fixed number of stripes (e.g., 256 locks by hash). No removal needed, no leak. 

Use `Object[] stripes = new Object[256]`; lock = stripes[(rid.hashCode() & 0x7fffffff) % 256]. Collisions just serialize unrelated requests briefly. 

But the lock is still released before tx commit (execute returns, then service method returns, then commit). So same-requestId concurrent requests: B waits on stripe, enters after A's execute returns but possibly before A's tx commits. B does findById → not visible → insertPlaceholder → blocks on DB row lock (if H2 blocks) until A commits → DuplicateKeyException → replay. If H2 throws immediately → retry loop handles it. Either way OK. Good — DB backstop + retry.

